// Constant-time comparison of secret octets.
//
// API
// ---
//   consttime.is_equal(left, right)   -> bool
//
// `bytes`'s own `==` (reference §3.10) makes no promise about its timing, and
// a MAC, tag or Finished check whose time leaks *where* the first mismatched
// octet was is a forgery oracle -- the class of bug RFC 8439 §3 warns about.
// Anything that compares an authenticator against a value the peer chose uses
// this instead. Public data (a certificate, a host key, a session id) has no
// use for it and keeps `==`.
//
// The time taken depends on the two lengths and on nothing else. Lengths are
// public in every protocol this serves, so unequal lengths answer `false` at
// once: that is not a channel, and there is nothing to accumulate over.

/// Whether `left` and `right` hold the same octets, taking time that depends
/// on their lengths and not on where they first differ. Two empty strings are
/// equal; strings of different lengths never are.
pub bool is_equal(bytes left, bytes right) {
    if (left.size() != right.size()) {
        return false;
    }
    int difference = 0;
    for (int index in 0 .. left.size()) {
        difference = difference | (left[index] ^ right[index]);
    }
    return difference == 0;
}
